@Screen editor
The screen editor allows to edit screen layouts. The initial mode is normal
mode; some commands change modes, as described below.

The possible editing modes are:
!norm;Normal mode / draw mode
!mass;Mass mode
!text;Text mode
!bloc;Block mode

Others:
!mark;Marks
!-screen;Screen elements
!info;Screen info
!colon;Colon commands
!-editwin;Window info editor

:norm;Normal mode / draw mode
ESC        Exit
TAB        Toggle normal/draw mode
^V         Toggle view mode
0-9        Numeric prefix
ALT+I      Screen info
ALT+L      Set message left edge
ALT+M      Set message center position
ALT+R      Set message right edge
ALT+V      Set viewport center position
ALT+W      Window info
ALT+Z      Maximum numeric prefix
SPACE      Toggle mark
c          Select drawing colour
C          Change colour of tile at cursor
d          Delete tile at cursor
e          Edit tile at cursor
h j k l    Move cursor (arrows can also be used)
H J K L    Move cursor and extend numeric display
i          Invert mark
m          Mass mode
n          Find next mark
N          Find previous mark
p          Place tile
r          Reverse background/foreground colour
t          Text entry
u          Unmark all
v          Block mode
y          Copy tile at cursor (place with p)
z          Far movement prefix
 :         Colon commands
F1         Menu: Backgrounds, Tiles, Windows
F2         Menu: Indicators, Bits
F3         Menu: Numbers (status variables)
F4         Menu: Numbers (specials), Memory

Draw mode will cause it to automatically place (like the "p" command)
as you move the cursor; normal mode doesn't do that.

:mass;Mass mode
c          Set colour of all marked (discard marks)
C          Set colour of all marked (keep marks)
p          Place at all marked (discard marks)
P          Place at all marked (keep marks)
h j k l    Paint copies of marked cells (arrows can also be used)

:text;Text mode
In this mode, you can type any text that you want to enter. In addition,
the following keys can also be used:

ESC        Leave text mode; switch to normal mode
^P         Enter special character

:bloc;Block mode
Block mode is used for selecting a rectangular block.

SPACE      Toggle marks in selected block; leave block mode
RETURN     Mark selected block and cancel
b          Draw thin border; leave block mode
B          Draw thick border; leave block mode
m          Mark selected block and switch to mass mode
;          Move cursor to opposite corner
ALT+H      Set hard edges
ALT+S      Set soft edges

!edge;Edges

:mark;Marks

Marks can be placed in the grid to perform batch operations with mass mode
and with colon commands. These marks are not saved in the world file; they
are only used in the editor, and are lost if you quit from Super ZZ Zero.

The mark grid is kept in memory as long as Super ZZ Zero is still running,
but only one mark grid is kept for all screen layouts; if you make marks
and then edit a different screen, the marks will appear on the new screen
instead (to avoid this, push "u" when entering the screen editor).

:info;Screen info

"Screen name" is a title for the game author's use; it is not used for any
purpose during the game, and is only displayed in the editor.

The "view center" is used when recentering the view, which is done
automatically upon entering a board; the view will be scrolled so that
the player (stat 1:0) appears at the specified screen coordinates.

The message position defines where the message line should appear. If the
Y coordinate is set to 25 then it will not be displayed. The message X
should be the X coordinate where the message text will be centered, but it
will be adjusted to fit within the boundaries if necessary; no text will
be displayed outside of these boundaries.

The border characters are used for displaying the borders at the specified
edges of the board or other things, according to other settings if the
border display has been enabled. Set these to zero to suppress the borders.

"Left align message" will left align the message text if selected. If not
selected, it is centered at the message X coordinate.

"Flashy message" will cause message text to automatically change colours.
If not selected, the colour at those coordinates in the screen layout will
be used to display the message text instead. This is not meaningful for
text windows, which will always use the colour at the coordinates.

"Exit indicators use border character" and "User indicators use border
character" will cause the border characters to be used instead of blank
characters if those indicators appear in the screen layout but what the
indicator refers to should be "off" instead of "active". For text windows,
these instead control the use of borders with scroll indicators.

"Disable scrolling" will disable automatic scrolling if selected. In this
case, the northwest corner of the board is placed at the hard edges.

"Message edging" will add character zero (normally displayed as a blank
space) on the left and right edge of the message text when it is displayed.

In addition, there is a submenu for dealing with edges.
:edge
"Soft edges" are the soft limits where the player can be inside of; if the
player steps out of range, the camera moves so that the player is in range.

"Hard edges" are the limits for where the board grid can be placed inside
of. These override the soft edges if the camera would move such that the
outside of the board would be within the hard edge limits.

Note that the screen layout can have board tiles outside of the hard edges,
and they will still be displayed. The hard edges are only used to restrict
the movement of the camera, not where the board is actually displayed.

For text windows, the east/west hard edges are where the text is aligned
and the north/south hard edges are the extents of the view, and the soft
edges are used for aligning the text of links.

:colon;Colon commands
These colon commands can be used for various batch operations.

Most of these commands allow a prefix; this can be & for marked cells only,
~& for unmarked cells only, % for all cells, . for the cell at the cursor,
or one or two coordinate pairs. If there are coordinate pairs, put : in
between. Each coordinate pair otherwise uses two numbers with a comma in
between, being the zero-based X and Y coordinates. If a number is preceded
by - or + then it is relative to the cursor.

Many colon commands have abbreviations; these are listed after the slash in
the below listings of the commands.

color/c
  Set the colour of cells. This can either be the current colour or a
  colour specified as a hexadecimal colour code.

export/ex
  Export the current screen layout to a file. If the file name starts with
  | then it is instead a pipe to an external shell command.

import/im
  Import the current screen layout from a file. If the file name starts with
  | then it is instead a pipe from an external shell command.

mark/m
  Marks cells.

markonly/mo
  Marks cells, but unmarks any cells that are not being marked in this way.

memory/mem
  Requires an argument being a starting memory address, in decimal, or in
  hexadecimal with $ at first. This command expects a range prefix to be
  given; it will fill the area with memory tiles that refer to the memory
  address, starting at the specified address and then advancing to the
  right, and the left side of the next row continues where it left off, etc.

mzmimport/mzmim
  Import a MZM file; if the file name starts with | then it is a pipe from an
  external shell command. The area to operate in must match the dimensions of
  the MZM file.

place/p
  Place copies of the tile selected with the "y" normal command.

status
  Toggles the status line display. You can specify 0 or 1 to disable or
  enable the status line display, respectively; if neither is specified,
  then the current state is toggled.

toggle/t
  Toggles the marks of cells.

unmark/u
  Unmarks cells.

